      <h1>Zanzibar Visa Guide 2025: e-Visa, Visa on Arrival & Requirements</h1>
      <p class="blog-excerpt">Everything you need to know about Zanzibar visa requirements for 2025. From e-Visa applications to visa on arrival, fees, exemptions, and the complete step-by-step process for every nationality.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="blog-content">
      <section>
        <h2>Do You Need a Visa for Zanzibar?</h2>
        <p>Zanzibar is part of Tanzania, so <strong>Tanzania's visa policy applies</strong>. Most visitors need a visa, but the good news is the process is straightforward with multiple options available.</p>
        
        <div class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin:2rem 0;background:#f8fafc;border-left:4px solid #00667c;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">✅ Visa-Free Countries</h3>
          <p>Citizens of these countries can enter Tanzania (including Zanzibar) <strong>visa-free for up to 90 days</strong>:</p>
          <ul style="columns:2;margin:1rem 0;">
            <li>All East African Community (EAC) members: Kenya, Uganda, Rwanda, Burundi, South Sudan, DRC</li>
            <li>SADC members: South Africa, Botswana, Namibia, Zambia, Zimbabwe, Malawi, Mozambique, Eswatini, Lesotho</li>
            <li>Others: Hong Kong, Malaysia, Singapore, Barbados, Jamaica, Cyprus, Romania, Malaysia</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Option 1: Tanzania e-Visa (Recommended)</h2>
        <p>The <strong>official Tanzania e-Visa portal</strong> (visa.immigration.go.tz) is the most reliable way to get your visa before travel. Apply at least 2 weeks before departure.</p>
        
        <h3>e-Visa Requirements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Passport valid for 6+ months beyond entry date</li>
          <li>Digital passport photo (white background, 2×2 inches)</li>
          <li>Return/onward flight ticket</li>
          <li>Hotel booking confirmation or invitation letter</li>
          <li>Yellow fever certificate (if arriving from endemic country)</li>
          <li>Credit/debit card for payment</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>e-Visa Fees (2025)</h3>
        <table class="rate-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Visa Type</th><th>Fee (USD)</th><th>Validity</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Single Entry Tourist</td><td>$50</td><td>90 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Multiple Entry Tourist</td><td>$100</td><td>12 months</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transit Visa</td><td>$30</td><td>7 days</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        
        <p style="font-size:.9rem;color:#4e5d66;">Fees are non-refundable. Processing: 3-10 business days.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Option 2: Visa on Arrival</h2>
        <p>Available at <strong>Abeid Amani Karume International Airport (ZNZ)</strong> and other entry points. Cash (USD) preferred; cards sometimes accepted.</p>
        
        <div class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;background:#fff3e0;border-left:4px solid #ff9800;">
          <h3 style="margin-top:0;">⚠️ Visa on Arrival Caveats</h3>
          <ul style="margin:0;">
            <li>Longer queues (30-90 min at peak)</li>
            <li>Cash only in clean, undamaged USD notes (post-2013 series)</li>
            <li>Exact change not always available</li>
            <li>Not available for all nationalities (check eligibility)</li>
            <li>System outages can cause delays</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Option 3: East Africa Tourist Visa</h2>
        <p>If you're also visiting Kenya and/or Uganda, the <strong>East Africa Tourist Visa ($100)</strong> covers all three countries for 90 days. Apply via Kenya's e-Visa portal or on arrival at Nairobi/JKIA.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Yellow Fever Certificate</h2>
        <p><strong>Required ONLY if you're arriving from (or transited >12 hours through) a yellow fever endemic country.</strong> This includes most of sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South America. If you're flying direct from Europe, North America, Asia, or the Middle East, you do NOT need it.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Passport Requirements</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Valid for <strong>6+ months</strong> beyond your planned departure from Tanzania</li>
          <li>At least <strong>2 blank pages</strong> for stamps</li>
          <li>No damage, tears, or missing pages</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Step-by-Step: Applying for the e-Visa</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Go to <a href="https://visa.immigration.go.tz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">visa.immigration.go.tz</a></li>
          <li>Create an account with email</li>
          <li>Select "Ordinary Visa" → "Single Entry" or "Multiple Entry"</li>
          <li>Fill in personal details exactly as in passport</li>
          <li>Upload passport bio page scan + passport photo</li>
          <li>Enter travel dates, flight details, Zanzibar accommodation</li>
          <li>Pay by Visa/Mastercard (3D Secure)</li>
          <li>Receive approval via email (PDF) — print 2 copies</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Can I extend my visa in Zanzibar?</summary><p>Yes, visit the Immigration Office in Stone Town (Malindi area). Extensions up to 90 days possible; fee applies. Apply before current visa expires.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if my e-Visa is rejected?</summary><p>You'll receive a reason. Common issues: passport expiry <6 months, photo doesn't meet specs, incomplete info. You can reapply or get visa on arrival if eligible.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do children need a separate visa?</summary><p>Yes, every traveler needs their own visa regardless of age. Children's passports must also be valid 6+ months.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is Zanzibar visa different from Tanzania visa?</summary><p>No. Zanzibar is part of Tanzania. One Tanzania visa covers both mainland and Zanzibar.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I enter Tanzania mainland with a Zanzibar visa on arrival?</summary><p>Yes. The visa on arrival at ZNZ is a Tanzania visa valid for both Zanzibar and mainland Tanzania.</p></details>
      </section>

      <div class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin-top:2rem;background:#f0f9ff;border-left:4px solid #00667c;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">💡 Pro Tip from Spice & Sail</h3>
        <p style="margin:0;">Apply for the e-Visa 3-4 weeks before travel. It saves hours at the airport and guarantees entry. We can remind you of the exact link and requirements when you book your trip with us.</p>
      </div>
